{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:c38ffbcc4f736b3a7f0874739ceb72dd4d2b164442ae71736215a04a05b3fe60","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.8},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.55}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"WEIGHT RECONCILIATION FIRST, because it is worth more than any AI story. 2025 PPP: OECD-38 ~$76T, China ~$41T, bloc $117T -> shares 0.650/0.350. But the question averages growth over 2032-2036, so the operative weights are MID-WINDOW REAL LEVELS, not 2025 levels: OECD at ~1.0%/yr to 2032 -> $81.5T, then $83T by 2034; China at ~3.8%/yr -> $53.3T by 2032, $57T by 2034. Mid-window weights = OECD 0.593 / China 0.407. ALL THREE WAVE-1 PANELISTS USED CHINA AT 0.32-0.37 (0.37 Scout, 0.32 Modeler, 0.34 Auditor) — a 6-8pp understatement worth +0.06 to +0.12pp on the answer. I use 0.60/0.40.\nOECD-38 2032-36. Bottom-up: labour input = working-age -0.30%/yr + participation/retirement-age extension +0.25%/yr = -0.05%/yr x labour share 0.60 = -0.03pp. Capital deepening: investment 22% of GDP, depreciation 15% -> net K +1.3%/yr x capital share 0.40 = +0.52pp, plus +0.02pp for the AI/grid stock, minus 0.10pp expected post-bust investment weakness (AI-Q4 at 0.66 x -0.15pp) = +0.44pp. TFP: AI-Q6 expectation = 0.162x0.70 + 0.472x0.35 + 0.365x0.05 = 0.113+0.165+0.018 = +0.296pp above baseline; baseline OECD TFP ~+0.30 -> +0.60pp. Bottom-up GDP = -0.03+0.44+0.60 = 1.01%/yr. RECONCILIATION TO THE LOCKED PRIOR: 2A locks per-capita 0.6%/yr absent offsets; +0.15%/yr population = 0.75% absent offsets; +0.296 AI TFP, -0.10 post-bust investment, -0.10 for the tax-wedge/repression misallocation my own coupling channel imposes = 0.85%/yr. The two routes differ by 0.16pp because my capital+baseline-TFP terms imply 0.82% per capita against 2A's locked 0.60%; 2A is locked, so I split toward it: OECD-38 = 0.90%/yr.\nCHINA 2032-36, published. Labour: working-age -1.3%/yr + reform participation +0.3 = -1.0%/yr x labour share 0.45 = -0.45pp. Capital: investment 38%->32%, K/Y ~3.15, depreciation ~6.5% of K = 20.5% of GDP -> net K growth (34-20.5)/315 falling through the window, average ~+4.0%/yr x capital share 0.50 = +2.00pp, haircut for ICOR deterioration as the marginal project turns from housing to grid/DC. TFP: 2015-22 baseline ~+0.8, automation demand-pull +0.5 (the world's strongest case: worker/retiree 4.4->2.8 with a domestic wage signal), minus 0.4 for pension-subsidy crowding of public investment (10.1%->15%+ of expenditure) = +0.90pp. Sum = -0.45+2.00+0.90 = 2.45%/yr TRUE; plus a +0.5 to +0.7pp published-print/deflator wedge = ~3.05%/yr PUBLISHED. Independent check against the lock: China 2015-24 realized ~5.8%/yr minus 2A's -2.7pp aging drag = 3.1%/yr. Two routes converge at 3.05-3.10.\nCOMBINED P50 = 0.60x0.90 + 0.40x3.05 = 0.540 + 1.220 = 1.760 -> 1.80, nudged for the weight continuing to drift to ~0.42 by 2036.\nTHE WEIGHT-TIMES-DRAG INTERACTION THE BRIEF ASKS FOR, EXPLICITLY: China at 0.40 x -2.7pp = -1.08pp of coupled growth from Chinese aging alone; OECD at 0.60 x -0.4pp (1.0->0.6 per capita) = -0.24pp. Total aging drag on THIS bloc = -1.32pp, i.e. LARGER than 2A's global -1.1pp, because the bloc is the old half of the world and the higher China weight makes the drag worse at the same time as it raises the growth level. The two effects partially offset, which is why the panel's lower China weights happened to land near the right answer for the wrong reason.\nDISPERSION: component sigmas OECD +/-0.55, China +/-0.90, correlation +0.4. sigma = sqrt((0.6x0.55)^2 + (0.4x0.90)^2 + 2x0.4x0.33x0.36) = sqrt(0.109+0.130+0.095) = 0.578. P10 = 1.80 - 1.282x0.578 = 1.06, shaded to 1.00 for recession left-skew. P90 = 1.80 + 1.282x0.578 = 2.54 -> 2.55. P(g>=2.6) = 1-Phi(1.384) = 0.083, allowed to 0.095 for the AI-Q1 Breakout right tail.\nPANELIST FLAGS. (1) The Resolution Auditor is internally inconsistent across his own two answers: his CQ3 Fermi uses China's published print at 3.2%, but his CQ6-A arithmetic uses 4.0% (\"(2.6 - 0.34x4.0)/0.66 = 1.88\"). At his OWN CQ3 central of 3.2%, a 2.6% bloc requires OECD growth of (2.6-1.088)/0.66 = 2.29%/yr, far above the 1.88% he quotes — so his A=11 is too HIGH by his own numbers, not too low. (2) The Mechanism Modeler's P90 of 2.65 does not follow from his stated corner: 0.68x2.1 + 0.32x3.6 = 2.58, not 2.65. (3) The Scout computes 0.63x1.2 + 0.37x3.0 = 1.866 and then reports P50 = 1.95, an unexplained +8bp; and with his own stated P50 1.95 / sigma 0.74, P(g>=2.6) = 0.190, not the 0.16 he carries into CQ6-A.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.699Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"G7 GDP-weighted 10y real yield (PPP-weighted, computed on the same seven benchmarks) averages below 1.00% for four consecutive quarters, with at least one G7 sovereign having publicly adopted explicit yield-curve control, a captive-buyer mandate for banks or insurers, or a formal duration-shortening program.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ4 repression branch has fired early and is binding on the G7-weighted measure, so its probability was well above 0.40. CQ4 P50 moves down from 1.95 to ~1.35 and P90 down from 3.05 to ~2.30; the variance attribution flips from 65% branch-switch to premium-dominated because the branch is resolved. CQ5 conjunct A falls (repression suppresses realized rate vol), taking CQ5 from 26 to ~17. CQ6-C gains ~5pp from B (repression runs inflation above nominal yields), and CQ3 loses ~0.10pp to misallocation.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative announced-and-cancelled or impaired hyperscaler AI buildout reaches 20% of announced capacity, OR net external AI software/API revenue stays below 0.5x annualized AI compute D&A for two consecutive reported years — i.e. AI-Q4 resolves YES — AND MSCI World does NOT fall 25% peak-to-trough within four quarters of the second confirming disclosure.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The bust happens without the equity event, which breaks the coupling my CQ5 conjunct B leans on: P(>=25% drawdown 2027-36) drops from 0.74 to ~0.55 and CQ5 falls from 26 to ~18. It also moves the bust INSIDE my CQ3 window as a pure investment drag without a demand collapse: CQ3 P50 down ~0.10 to 1.70, CQ6-D up ~4pp from B. If instead the drawdown DOES arrive with bonds rallying 100bp+, CQ5 falls further, to ~14.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD publishes a GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation aggregate for 2027-2029 that averages below 2.20% on the ALL-MEMBERS basis (Turkiye and Mexico included), not merely on an ex-Turkiye basis.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My Turkiye/Mexico weighting arithmetic — the single driver of my CQ2 being 4-19pp above my panel — has been overtaken by Turkish disinflation or a basket re-weighting. CQ2 falls from 37 to ~22, CQ6-C falls from 16 to ~11, CQ6-D rises from 11 to ~15, and the remaining ~1pp goes to B. My P(core 2032-36 >= 2.70) falls from 0.30 to ~0.18.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's published annual real GDP growth prints below 2.5% in any two consecutive calendar years, with the GDP deflator non-negative in both.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the 3.05%/yr published China path that supplies 1.22pp of my 1.80% coupled central, and removes the print-wedge cushion. CQ3 P50 falls from 1.80 to ~1.45 and P10 to ~0.70; CQ6 shifts to roughly A 6 / B 55 / C 15 / D 24, because the g<1.7 band grows and the bust branch dominates it. It also strengthens my CQ2 conjunct B (China deflation), raising CQ2 by ~2pp, and lowers r* by ~8bp via a persisting Chinese saving surplus, taking CQ4 P50 to ~1.85.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat SES 2030 wave (published ~2033) or UK ASHE and Canadian LFS annual medians show NACE F+Q real median hourly earnings outpacing J+K+M by more than 5.0pp cumulative over 2025-2030 in a majority of the members that publish both.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my quantified composition offsets (-12 to -16pp from Q entrants, +12 to +15pp from J/K/M truncation) that drive the central measured gap to -3pp. On a 5pp-by-2030 run rate the 10pp-by-2036 bar becomes reachable: P(CQ1 conjunct 2) rises from 0.10 to ~0.35 and CQ1 from 8 to ~22. It would also raise my care-wage pass-through assumption from 25-35% toward 50%, adding ~0.15pp to OECD core inflation and moving ~4pp of CQ6 mass from B into C.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 fermi seat, effort 'rstar'."}}